#    core 3.5. Released in SDK 2.0.0. ──
#
# health=2.6 / clinical=1.14: interpretation is no longer a five-word set this
#     project invented. LAB_INTERPRETATION_VALUES carries the 49 SELECTABLE codes
#     of HL7 v3 ObservationInterpretation 3.0.0 (the code system FHIR R4 binds
#     Observation.interpretation to) in the code system's own order, plus the
#     data-absent-reason code "unknown" and the ten retained legacy words: 60
#     values. LabInterpretation is DERIVED from that array, so the type and the
#     runtime list cannot drift. The list is pinned by a SHA-256 recomputed in
#     test rather than by reading the shape file, because this package's CI
#     checks out no spec sibling and a test that skipped itself when its input
#     was missing would report green while proving nothing.
#     Two pre-existing defects are corrected by the same change: "elevated" was a
#     member of the union and was never accepted by any version of the shapes,
#     and "high" was accepted by every version and was missing from the union.
# health=2.6 / clinical=1.14: labCategory, testCode, icd10Code and snomedCode are
#     0..*. FHIR R4 Observation.category is 0..* and CodeableConcept.coding is
#     0..*, so a record that preserved every coding its source sent was being
#     rejected for preserving it. Represented as MultiValue<string> (a bare value
#     or an array) and handled ARITY-PRESERVINGLY on both sides: N values in
#     produce N repeated-predicate triples, N triples read back as N values, and
#     one value still round-trips as a bare string. Uniform by FIELD NAME across
#     every model that carries one of the four, because the serializer resolves
#     the namespace per record type but decides cardinality before it has any
#     type context, and no shape still caps any of the four at one.
#     NOT an rdf:List: the vocabulary declares no order and the shapes count
#     triples. The JSON-LD context keeps these terms plain rather than declaring
#     @container: @set, because @set means "always an array on compaction" and
#     this SDK emits a bare value for a single coding.
# coverage=1.4: SubscriberRelationship completed to the full HL7
#     SubscriberPolicyholder code system that FHIR R4 Coverage.relationship binds
#     to (child, parent, spouse, common, other, self, injured). v1.4 added common
#     and injured; parent had been missing here since the type was introduced.
#     The other v1.4 change relaxes coverage:coverageType from a closed list to an
#     advisory warning, matching FHIR's EXTENSIBLE binding; this SDK already typed
#     that field `CoverageType | string` and rejects nothing, so it needed no
#     change to be conformant.
# checkup=3.3: SHACL only. An icd10Code sh:pattern that required a digit in the
#     second and third characters, so live categories with a letter there (C4A,
#     D3A, M1A) failed. This SDK validates no lexical patterns and models no
#     checkup: class, so there is nothing here for the fix to change. The row is
#     bumped because 3.3 adds no term this SDK is missing, not because code moved.
# core=3.5: cascade:sourceIdentity, the ORIGIN axis: a canonical,
#     transport-independent identity for the organization a record came from, so
#     a FHIR export and a C-CDA document of one health system agree. Registered
#     as a predicate, carried in the generated JSON-LD context as a plain term
#     (matching the published context), and typed on CascadeEntity because its
#     rdfs:domain is owl:Thing. Values are scheme-prefixed org: / ns: /
#     transport:; the accessor is a plain optional string and this SDK does NOT
#     validate or derive the scheme. Minting a slug requires the source document,
#     which only an importer has, and rejecting an unrecognized value here would
#     discard origin information a producer was entitled to write.
#     cascade:sourceSystem, whose comment narrowed in the same version, is still
#     unregistered here and remains a gap.

# core=3.6: cascade:dataAbsentReason registered and in the generated context;
#     the serializer dropped it entirely before. cascade:sourceSystem
#     registered too: it has been in the published context since core v3.0 and
#     was never registered here, so the INGESTION axis could not round-trip
#     while ORIGIN could.
# health=2.7 / clinical=1.15: interpretationSourceCode registered in BOTH
#     spellings (health: on labs, clinical: on vitals) and typed on VitalSign
#     AND on LabResult, as MultiValue<string>. The arity is the writer's and the
#     reader's: a triple per value out, every triple back, so a model saying
#     `string` described data this SDK does not produce and a caller's
#     `.trim()` typechecked and threw. sh:maxCount 1 on both shapes is a rule
#     about validating a graph, not about what a reader may find in one.
#     LAB_INTERPRETATION_VALUES moves 60 to 74 (the 14 data-absent-reason codes
#     it lacked) and the checksum is re-pinned. VitalSign.interpretation
#     narrows from `VitalInterpretation | string`, which TypeScript collapsed
#     to `string`: clinical v1.15 binds the vital shape, so the open binding
#     that justified the union is gone. Procedure records retarget onto
#     clinical:Procedure / clinical:procedureName, with health:procedureName
#     registered so a name carried only on the deprecated spelling survives.

# ── 2026-08-27 wave-4 sync: core 3.7, health 2.8, clinical 1.16,
#    coverage 1.5. All 24 terms implemented. ──
#
# core=3.7: pod attachments. cascade:Attachment plus all seven properties
#     (hasAttachment, attachmentPath, attachmentMediaType, contentHash,
#     hashAlgorithm, byteSize, attachmentTitle), a model, a TYPE_MAPPING entry,
#     full serialize/deserialize and JSON-LD context coverage.
#     AN ATTACHMENT IS A SUBJECT WITH ITS OWN IRI, NOT AN INLINE SUB-NODE.
#     cascade:HasAttachmentEdgeShape declares sh:nodeKind sh:IRI, so the record
#     and the attachment can live in different files; a blank node would fail
#     the shape. hasAttachment is therefore an IRI edge (URI_FIELDS +
#     IRI_ARRAY_FIELDS), 0..*, and its context term is @type: @id with
#     @container: @set. byteSize is the only typed literal (xsd:integer).
#     THE BYTES ARE NOT MODELLED. Cascade stores them as an ordinary
#     content-addressed file and this SDK touches only the metadata node: it
#     neither reads, writes, hashes nor verifies attachment bytes, and does not
#     validate that attachmentPath is pod-relative or that contentHash is
#     lowercase hex. Those are sh:Violation constraints in core.shapes.ttl and
#     this package validates no lexical patterns.
# clinical=1.16: fifteen terms. The eight flat encounter facts
#     (encounterClassDisplay, encounterClassSystem, encounterReason 0..*,
#     admitSource, dischargeDisposition) and the participation structure
#     (EncounterParticipant, hasParticipant, participantName, participantRole,
#     participantRoleCode 0..*, participantSpecialty) are on the Encounter
#     model; businessIdentifier is on CascadeEntity because the ontology
#     deliberately declares no rdfs:domain for it.
#     A PARTICIPATION IS AN INLINE BLANK NODE — the opposite ruling from
#     core v3.7's attachment, and deliberately so.
#     clinical:EncounterParticipantShape omits sh:nodeKind sh:IRI precisely so
#     a serializer may write one for a structural sub-node, since nothing points
#     at a participation from another file. Modelled on the core v3.4
#     clinicalSummary / wellnessSummary precedent (BLANK_NODE_TYPES plus
#     reconstruction on read), extended in two ways that precedent did not need:
#     the edge REPEATS, so an array of blank nodes is written and every one is
#     rebuilt on read; and the nested predicates are qualified per parent field
#     (BLANK_NODE_PREDICATE_PREFIXES), because the writer hardcoded cascade:.
#     A blanket PROPERTY_PREDICATES lookup was rejected: a `name` inside an
#     emergencyContact is cascade:name while the top-level `name` is foaf:name,
#     so it would have silently rewritten stable output.
#     encounterClassSystem is written as a PLAIN STRING LITERAL although the
#     ontology ranges it xsd:anyURI; clinical:EncounterShape accepts anyURI OR
#     string via sh:or, explicitly because serializers differ on which they
#     write for a URI-valued literal.
#     businessIdentifier values are the FHIR token form "{system}|{value}" where
#     the source states a system. This SDK round-trips the string verbatim and
#     does NOT split, parse or validate it, and cannot invent a system.
# clinical=1.16: the three document terms (documentReferenceStatus,
#     documentAuthorName, authenticatorName) are registered as PREDICATES ONLY.
#     Their rdfs:domain is clinical:ClinicalDocument, a class this SDK does not
#     model, so there is nothing here to attach them to — the same position the
#     core v3.4 device-source terms (sourceType, dataTypes, version) are in.
#     They serialize and deserialize on whatever subject a caller writes them
#     to; leaving them unregistered would have dropped them silently. A
#     ClinicalDocument model is the follow-up.
#     The two DOMAINS DROPPED in v1.16 (providerName off CoverageRecord,
#     verificationStatus off Condition) and the ONE DEPRECATION
#     (clinical:observationStatus) need no change here: this SDK encodes no
#     domains, and observationStatus was never registered.
# coverage=1.5: coverage:status, the four fm-status codes, typed as the closed
#     union CoverageStatus. Closed rather than `CoverageStatus | string` for the
#     reason clinical v1.15 closed VitalSign.interpretation: the FHIR binding is
#     REQUIRED and the shape constrains the value at sh:Violation. Contrast
#     coverageType, which stays open because Coverage.type is bound EXTENSIBLY.
#     WRITTEN VIA A RECORD-TYPE OVERRIDE, not a PROPERTY_PREDICATES row: the
#     `status` key already resolves to health:status. The override is declared
#     for 'InsurancePlan' ONLY and not for 'CoverageRecord', because
#     coverage:status has rdfs:domain coverage:InsurancePlan and asserting it on
#     a clinical:CoverageRecord subject would entail to a reasoner that the
#     subject is an InsurancePlan.
#     KNOWN GAP, pre-existing and NOT introduced here: TYPE_MAPPING resolves
#     both 'InsurancePlan' and 'CoverageRecord' to rdfType
#     clinical:CoverageRecord, so this SDK cannot emit a coverage:InsurancePlan
#     subject at all and coverage's own shapes never see these records. The
#     practical consequence is that coverage:status is a ONE-WAY trip: the class
#     is lost on read, so re-serializing what came back writes health:status.
#     Pinned by test rather than fixed, because retargeting the class changes
#     what every existing InsurancePlan record serializes as.
# health=2.8: SHACL only; no class or property added, removed or renamed.
#     health:LabResultRecordShape gains clinical:status bound to the 8 codes of
#     Observation.status, and health:AllergyRecordShape gains clinical:status
#     and clinical:verificationStatus bound to the AllergyIntolerance sets.
#     This SDK validates no value sets, so the row is bumped because 2.8 adds no
