#     package validates neither value sets nor datatypes, so neither version
#     asks anything of this code and claiming them would claim support it did
#     not gain. The row stays at 1.5 and the drift against spec is real.
#     What DID change is that a coverage shape can now see a record this SDK
#     wrote at all; until this release every one of them was unreachable by
#     construction.
#
# THE GAP THE coverage=1.5 ENTRY ABOVE RECORDED IS CLOSED. That entry called
#     it "KNOWN GAP, pre-existing and NOT introduced here": TYPE_MAPPING
#     resolved both 'InsurancePlan' and 'CoverageRecord' to rdfType
#     clinical:CoverageRecord, so this SDK could not emit a
#     coverage:InsurancePlan subject at all and coverage's own shapes never saw
#     these records. coverage:status was therefore a one-way trip — written
#     correctly, the class lost on read, health:status written on the way back
#     out. TYPE_MAPPING.insurance now carries rdfType coverage:InsurancePlan
#     and namePred coverage:providerName, and the round trip holds in both
#     directions.
#
# ELEVEN DEFECTS, NOT ONE, and they had to move together. The class was one of
#     them. Eight were predicates: providerName, memberId, groupNumber,
#     planName, planType, coverageType and subscriberId were written clinical:
#     where the corpus says coverage:, and sourceRecordId was written health:.
#     Two were datatypes: effectiveStart and effectiveEnd carried xsd:dateTime
#     where coverage.ttl:93,99 ranges xsd:date and coverage.shapes.ttl:146
#     declares sh:datatype xsd:date, so the time component was a midnight-UTC
#     placeholder.
#     FIXING ONLY THE CLASS WOULD HAVE BEEN WORSE THAN THE BUG.
#     coverage:InsurancePlanShape declares sh:minCount 1 at sh:Violation on
#     coverage:providerName, coverage:memberId and coverage:coverageType.
#     Retyping the subject while still writing clinical: makes the shape see
#     these records for the first time and report three violations plus two
#     datatype violations, on records whose data is correct.
#     THE EIGHT ARE A PER-TYPE OVERRIDE, NOT A PROPERTY_PREDICATES REMAP.
#     sourceRecordId is carried by 35 fixtures across other record types and
#     stays health:sourceRecordId for every one of them; the other seven are
#     narrower but the reasoning is identical. They join coverage:status in
#     TYPE_PREDICATE_OVERRIDES.InsurancePlan, and the read side moves with them:
#     all eight coverage: URIs are in the deserializer's
#     ADDITIONAL_REVERSE_MAPPINGS, mapping to the same JSON keys the clinical:
#     and health: spellings already map to, so a pod written either way reads
#     back in full.
#     clinical:payorName IS NOT ONE OF THEM AND DOES NOT MOVE. Coverage has no
#     payor property distinct from coverage:providerName, which is
#     sh:maxCount 1, so it is a legitimate clinical predicate on a plan.
#     conformance's coverage-001 expects it under clinical: on a
#     coverage:InsurancePlan subject, and a blanket "clinical: -> coverage: on a
#     plan" rewrite passes coverage-002 and -003 and breaks it.
#
# clinical=1.16 (UNCHANGED): clinical:CoverageRecord is now READ AND NEVER
#     WRITTEN, the asymmetry DEPRECATED_TYPE_ALIASES already gave the four
#     clinical v1.13 classes. The class has been deprecated in favour of
#     coverage:InsurancePlan since clinical v1.5 (clinical.ttl:187) and every
#     release of this SDK since v1.3.0 wrote it, so the pods that need the read
#     side are the ones this SDK produced — refusing them would be a data-loss
#     bug dressed up as standards compliance. The clinical row does not move:
#     nothing here implements a newer clinical version, a long-standing
#     deprecation is simply observed.
#     Coverage['type'] narrows to 'InsurancePlan'. 'CoverageRecord' stays in
#     TYPE_TO_MAPPING_KEY so a caller holding the old JSON spelling can still
#     name it to deserialize(), and it resolves to coverage:InsurancePlan like
#     the other, so neither spelling can make this SDK write the deprecated
#     class.
#
